     "- [ ] `release/remaining-tasks-and-evidence/execute-compiler-preservation-fixtures` Execute compiler preservation fixtures\n\nStatus: Open\n\nEvidence baseline: [TEST2](../../docs/phase-14-testing-verification-and-conformance/191-test2-compiler-test-strategy.md)\n\nClosing evidence: Implement and execute the Execute compiler preservation fixtures runner against actual declared owner interfaces, retaining the exact implementation/compiler/source/fixture/profile/target and applicable runtime/backend/provider identities. Run the named passing and failing cases; missing evidence is failure for the claimed scope, while inapplicable cases are explicitly reported. Source testing/test2/cache-invalidation: Run incremental fixtures that change relevant source/dependency/profile/target/policy/compiler inputs and assert invalidated reuse plus rebuilding exactly affected graph nodes. Concrete acceptance: Mutate source, compiler, dependency, profile, target, policy, grants and other stage-relevant inputs individually; observe exactly the affected rebuild graph and fail a forged reuse result. Retain actual input/output and diagnostic identities plus positive and rejected-case artifacts; supplied success flags, proposal delivery and broad parent links are not executable evidence. Source testing/test2/development-feedback: Run focused accepted/rejected phase tests and affected pass composition during implementation, recording accurate source/compiler context without claiming proof certification; authoritative migration/release separately requires fresh provenance, stage equivalence and bootstrap evidence after input changes. Concrete acceptance: Change a pass input/output interface and execute focused and affected composition fixtures against current source; fail lost facts or false authoritative-proof labels, without requiring a fresh bootstrap admission merely to run development tests. Retain actual input/output and diagnostic identities plus positive and rejected-case artifacts; supplied success flags, proposal delivery and broad parent links are not executable evidence. Source testing/test2/diagnostic-match: Match compiler diagnostic code, span, profile, target and remediation with stable tool-usable output. Concrete acceptance: Change code, source span, category, profile, target or remediation independently; fail mismatches even if localized message text differs legitimately. Retain actual input/output and diagnostic identities plus positive and rejected-case artifacts; supplied success flags, proposal delivery and broad parent links are not executable evidence. Source testing/test2/generated-reentry: Execute generated-code test fixtures through the normal compiler pipeline and assert retained source/generated provenance. Concrete acceptance: Submit generated source that is well shaped but type/effect/profile/capability/safety illegal or lacks producer origins; normal pipeline validation must reject before downstream execution. Retain actual input/output and diagnostic identities plus positive and rejected-case artifacts; supplied success flags, proposal delivery and broad parent links are not executable evidence. Source testing/test2/optimization-soundness: Reject unsound compiler rewrites and remove safety checks only with evidence that preserves safe semantics. Concrete acceptance: Execute an unsound rewrite and proofless safety-check deletion alongside the valid rewrite; fail preservation and prevent a claimed optimization pass. Retain actual input/output and diagnostic identities plus positive and rejected-case artifacts; supplied success flags, proposal delivery and broad parent links are not executable evidence. Source testing/test2/plugin-boundaries: Exercise compiler plugin capability, profile and pass-boundary enforcement without ambient authority. Concrete acceptance: Try an ungranted plugin effect, a write outside its pass boundary, incompatible profile or undeclared transformed fact; the fixture must observe denial befo