     "- [ ] `stdlib/remaining-tasks-and-evidence/complete-schemaqueryworkflowaimetacryptoui-adapters` Complete schema/query/workflow/AI/meta/crypto/UI adapters\n\nStatus: Open\n\nEvidence baseline: [Module inventory](../../docs/phase-16-standard-library/README.md)\n\nClosing evidence: APIs consuming corresponding domain/runtime outcomes, with boundary failures exercised; STD10-STD13, STD15, STD18 and STD19 fixtures validate schemas before trusted decoding, canonical bytes, migration compatibility, parameterized queries, transaction commit/rollback/retry/isolation, durable workflow replay and in-flight migration, AI schema/taint/tool/review gates, generated-code rechecking, syntax context and pass invalidations, crypto lifecycle/hybrid binding/redaction, and typed UI events/render trust/accessibility/routes. Require actual provider execution and artifact consumers, not catalogue names or manifest presence. Reject each source-specific boundary failure in plan.md; unresolved provider, binding and equivalence decisions remain unassessed. STD15 requires actual syntax construction/inspection/transform APIs to preserve spans, hygiene, namespace, metadata and phase; C3 defines the underlying syntax representation and internal transform rules. Retain the library-call outcome separately from internal compiler transformations and public API publication. Explicit metadata updates may change the requested metadata while recording the change; they do not erase origin/hygiene/context or make stale facts current. Reject the following false substitutes: An internal compiler transform fixture does not prove the published syntax API actually calls it. Publishing an export or returning raw form data does not preserve syntax context. with-syntax-meta is an explicit update, not a promise that all metadata bytes are unchanged. The broad query outcome lowers/executes a typed query and validates rows/failures under declared transaction/provider semantics. STD11 additionally requires the standard transaction API to execute commit, rollback, retry and isolation/idempotency behavior. Retain that concrete lifecycle outcome under the existing adapters task; a provider transaction implementation may be shared but its invocation and observable transitions through the standard API require their own acceptance. Reject the following false substitutes: A parameterized SELECT returning typed rows proves no commit/rollback path. Transaction metadata or a named commit export does not execute the transaction. Retry must follow declared isolation, idempotency and provider failure policy; it is not an invented retry allowance. Retain the standard rendering boundary that distinguishes escaped text, trusted markup, binary media and native views, and enforces the selected sink's trust policy. Actual web DOM/render-event execution is one qualified consumer; native/terminal/media adapters have their declared target rules. Reuse actual adapter checks without treating a web-only unsafe-DOM fixture or public rendering API inventory as universal rendering conformance. Reject the following false substitutes: A typed render export alone does not reject unsafe markup at an actual sink. HTML escaping is not a universal native-view, media or terminal trust rule. An event/schema fixture does not establish the rendering kind distinction or adapter invocation. Decision criterion interface/domain/crypto-provider-binding: Bind cryptographic operations/parsers/randomness to declared algorithms, versions, parameters, secure randomness, capability, safe FFI and test-vector/audit contracts. Negative fixtures: Reject provider-chosen defaults, mismatched algorithm/mode/key/nonce/output policy, unavailable required secure entropy, ungranted FFI and unaudited experimental algorithms. Required outputs: Algorithm/provider/parameter/randomness manifest, known-answer vectors and authority/refusal records. Bind exact source/compiler/profile/target and actual API/provider/artifact identities; exercise accepted and rejected cases under the attached source conditions. Status Open; no execution acceptance is inferred. Decision criterion interface/domain/crypto-side-channel-validation: Validate constant-time/side-channel claims against declared timing/leakage policy and required analysis/audit evidence. Negative fixtures: Reject secret-dependent branch/access/timing/diagnostic leakage and optimizations that invalidate the declared constant-time policy. Required outputs: Algorithm-bound timing/leakage policy, independent analysis/audit and before/after optimization evidence. Bind exact source/compiler/profile/target and actual API/provider/artifact identities; exercise accepted and rejected cases under the attached source conditions. Status Open; no execution acceptance is inferred. Decision criterion interface/stdlib/crypto/agile-replacement: Perform policy-driven algorithm replacement without silently changing application data schemas, recording deliberate migration breaks. Negative fixtures: Reject generic default/secure/provider-chosen suites, silent data-schema changes or unrecorded migration breaks. Required outputs: Classical/PQ/hybrid replacement policy and schema-preservation or explicit-break migration fixtures. Bind exact source/compiler/profile/target and actual API/provider/artifact identities; exercise accepted and rejected cases under the attached source conditions. Status Open; no execution acceptance is inferred. Decision criterion interface/stdlib/crypto/algorithm-lifecycle-admission: Publish and enforce machine-readable algorithm-family/parameter/lifecycle policy, including restricted future/experimental and legacy read/verify cases. Negative fixtures: Reject missing algorithm/parameter/lifecycle metadata, unauthorized future/experimental algorithms and legacy uses outside read/verify policy. Required outputs: Machine-readable family/parameter/status policy and lifecycle acceptance/refusal matrix. Bind exact source/compiler/profile/target and actual API/provider/artifact identities; exercise accepted and rejected cases under the attached source conditions. Status Open; no execution acceptance is inferred. Decision criterion interface/stdlib/crypto/hybrid-establishment-binding: Bind classical and post-quantum components, keys/ciphertexts, transcript/provider context, combiner/KDF and failure/downgrade handling before deriving application keys. Negative fixtures: Reject mismatched components/keys/ciphertexts/transcript/provider/combiner/KDF, partial-failure success, downgrade or deriving application keys before binding verification. Required outputs: Hybrid vectors, transcript/combiner records and mismatch/failure/downgrade fixtures. Bind exact source/compiler/profile/target and actual API/provider/artifact identities; exercise accepted and rejected cases under the attached source conditions. Status Open; no execution acceptance is inferred. Decision criterion interface/stdlib/crypto/migration-inventory: Emit a complete algorithm-bearing surface inventory with replacement targets, deadlines, compatibility, verification and old-artifact disposition. Negative fixtures: Reject omitted algorithm-bearing surfaces, missing replacement/deadline/compatibility/verification policy or unresolved old-artifact disposition presented as migrated. Required outputs: Algorithm inventory and replacement/deadline/compatibility/verification/old-artifact migration report. Bind exact source/compiler/profile/target and actual API/provider/artifact identities; exercise accepted and rejected cases under the attached source conditions. Status Open; no execution acceptance is inferred. Decision criterion interface/stdlib/db/parameterized-query-construction: Construct a typed query with untrusted values in explicit bound parameters separate from query structure, retaining the result row-schema reference. Negative fixtures: Reject untrusted interpolation into query structure, unbound values and missing/mismatched result-row schema identity